```java
public class KeywordDemo {
    private int count = 10;            // valid
    private boolean isActive = true;  // 'isActive' valid; 'true' is a literal

    void demo() {
        var orders = java.util.List.of("A", "B"); // 'var' as a keyword (type inference)
        // int class = 5;     // ERROR: keyword
        // const int x = 5;   // ERROR: 'const' reserved but unused — use 'final'
        System.out.println(count + orders.size());
    }
}
```

This compiles cleanly on Java 17. Uncomment either error line and the build breaks instantly — proof that the rule is enforced at compile time, long before the program ever runs. The `var orders` line shows the same word acting as a keyword, while the commented `const` line shows a word that is reserved yet does nothing.

This is the single most common static mistake — a static method trying to read an instance field. The compiler stops it cold.

```java
class Account {
    double balance;            // instance field
    static double getBalance() {
        return balance;        // COMPILE ERROR: no object context in a static method
    }
}
```

The failure isn't a typo — it's structural. `getBalance()` is static, so it runs without any object, which means there's no `this` and therefore no `balance` to point at. *Whose* balance would it even return if you called it on the class? The compiler refuses because that question has no answer.

Keep the static method, but point it at static data, and the problem disappears.

```java
class Account {
    private static int count = 0;        // shared across all accounts
    Account() { count++; }
    static int totalAccounts() { return count; } // works — only static state
}
```

This compiles because `totalAccounts()` only touches `count`, which is itself static — class-level data that a class-level method is allowed to see. The fix for the broken version above is always one of two moves: make the field static, or pass an `Account` instance into the method so it has an object to read from.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private static int counter;            // shared
    private static final String BANK = "GlobalBank"; // static constant
    private final String accountNumber;

    static { counter = 1000; }             // runs once at class load

    public BankAccount() {
        this.accountNumber = BANK + "-" + (counter++);
    }
    public static int created() { return counter - 1000; } // static method
}
```

The `static {}` block runs exactly once when the JVM first loads `BankAccount`, before any account exists — so `counter` is already 1000 by the time the first constructor runs and starts handing out numbers like `GlobalBank-1000`. That ordering is why static initializers are the right tool for one-time setup you need done before anything else can use the class.

final on a *method* is more surgical than final on a class — it freezes one behavior while the class stays fully extendable.

```java
class PaymentProcessor {
    public final void log(String id) { System.out.println(id); } // can't override
    public void process(double amt) { }                          // can override
}
class CardProcessor extends PaymentProcessor {
    @Override public void process(double amt) { /* allowed */ }
    // public void log(String id) {} // COMPILE ERROR: log is final
}
```

Notice the class itself isn't final — `CardProcessor` extends it freely and customizes `process()`. Only `log()` is locked. That's the template-method sweet spot: open up the steps that are meant to vary, and nail down the one step that must stay identical for every subclass.

```java
public final class StringUtils {         // final: no subclasses
    private StringUtils() {}              // private constructor: no instances
    public static boolean isBlank(String s) {
        return s == null || s.isBlank();
    }
}
// StringUtils.isBlank("  ") -> true
```

Marking `StringUtils` final and giving it a private constructor is the textbook pattern for a pure utility class: no subclass can change its behavior, and no one can pointlessly instantiate it. The JDK's own `Math` and `Collections` are built exactly this way — final, all-static, and impossible to subclass or `new`.

Here is a runnable program that uses the entry point exactly as the JVM expects, including reading a command-line argument so you can see what `args` is for.

```java
public class Greeter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String who = (args.length > 0) ? args[0] : "World";
        System.out.println("Hello, " + who + "!");
    }
}
// Run: java Greeter        -> Hello, World!
// Run: java Greeter Alice  -> Hello, Alice!
```

Notice `args` is just a normal `String` array — when you run `java Greeter Alice`, the launcher packs `"Alice"` into `args[0]`, and with no arguments `args` is an empty array (never `null`). Everything the JVM needs to start is in that one method signature; the body is ordinary code.

The sneakiest version of this mistake compiles perfectly. The problem only surfaces the moment you try to run it.

```java
public class App {
    public void main(String[] args) {   // static removed
        System.out.println("Starting...");
    }
}
// javac App.java  -> compiles fine
// java App        -> Error: Main method is not static in class App
```

Without `static`, the JVM would need an `App` object to call `main` on — but it creates no objects at startup, so it has nothing to call the method on. The compiler doesn't care (a non-static `main` is a perfectly legal method); only the launcher does, which is why the failure is a runtime launch error, not a compile error.

Restore `static` and the contract is satisfied — the JVM can call `main` straight on the class with no instance involved.

```java
public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) {  // static restored
        System.out.println("Starting...");
    }
}
// java App  -> Starting...
```

The same logic explains the other failures: rename `main` to `start` and the launcher reports it can't find a `main` method, because it searches for that literal name. The signature is matched piece by piece, so every essential token has to be exactly right before your first line of code ever runs.
